emdash  Among [the] carts and [the] chariots, I control my horses with a firm hand (}{\i\f211 horses mine by-firm hand I-rule}{\f208 ).\par 8.\emdash  The small horse is pulling a large waggon and is proceeding slowly (}{\i\f211 waggon large it-hauls, and slowly advances}{\f208 ).\par 9.\emdash  I easily pass the horse and the waggon (}{\i\f211 horse waggon-and easily I-pass}{\f208 ).\par 10.\emdash  The large waggon is pulled slowly and is easily passed by me.\par 11.\emdash  The swift vehicle passes the slow vehicles.\par 12.\emdash  I look at the chest, the horse, the vehicle.\par 13.\emdash  (}{\i\f211 Is-looked-at}{\f208 ) The chest, the horse, the vehicle is looked at.\par 14.\emdash  We look at the chests, the horses, the vehicles.\par 15.\emdash  (}{\i\f211 Are-looked-at}{\f208 ) The chests, the horses, the vehicles are looked at.\par \par NOTES\par }{\b\f210 1. Colloc}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 re}{\f208  is a composite verb: }{\b\f210 cum}{\f208  (}{\i\f211 with}{\f208 ) + }{\b\f210 loc}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 re}{\f208  (}{\i\f211 place}{\f208 ), the }{\b\f210 m}{\f208  of }{\b\f210 cum}{\f208  being transformed into an }{\b\f210 l}{\f208  and the }{\b\f210 u}{\f208  into an }{\b\f210 o}{\f208  so that its pronunciation is easier. Present tense: }{\b\f210 c}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 lloco}{\f208 , }{\b\f210 c}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 llocas}{\f208 , etc.\par }{\b\f210 2. Ex}{\f208 i}{\b\f210 re}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 to leave}{\f208 , is composed of }{\b\f210 ex}{\f208  (}{\i\f211 out of \u8230\'c9}{\f208 ) and of i}{\b\f210 re}{\f208  (}{\i\f211 to go}{\f208 ); present tense e}{\b\f210 xeo}{\f208 , e}{\b\f210 xis}{\f208 , etc., an irregular verb like i}{\b\f210 re}{\f208 , which we already saw in lesson 5. Similarly }{\b\f210 ab}{\f208 i}{\b\f210 re }{\f208 = }{\b\f210 ab}{\f208  (}{\i\f211 away from}{\f208 ) + i}{\b\f210 re}{\f208 , in P 6.\par Latin uses composite words a lot. We shall point out to you later some techniques for better comprehending this process of construction.\par }{\b\f210 3. }{\f208 A}{\b\f210 rca}{\f208 , a}{\b\f210 rcae}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 a trunk}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 trunks}{\f208 , if it acts as the subject; a}{\b\f210 rcam}{\f208 , a}{\b\f210 rcas}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 a trunk}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 trunks}{\f208 , if it acts as the direct object. This variation }{\b\f210 a}{\f208 , }{\b\f210 ae}{\f208 , }{\b\f210 am}{\f208 , }{\b\f210 as}{\f208  is common to a large number of feminine nouns; similarly: e}{\b\f210 quus}{\f208 , e}{\b\f210 qui}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 a horse}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 horses}{\f208  (subject); e}{\b\f210 quum}{\f208 , e}{\b\f210 quos}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 a horse}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 horses}{\f208  (dir. obj.). These last forms correspond in general to masculine nouns.\par The neuter nouns are much nicer, since they have the same form in both functions: }{\b\f210 veh}{\f208 i}{\b\f210 culum}{\f208 , }{\b\f210 veh}{\f208 i}{\b\f210 cula}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 a vehicle}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 vehicles}{\f208  (subject or dir. obj.).\par Do not worry for the moment about the }{\b\f210 c}{\f208 u}{\b\f210 rrus}{\f208  in P 7 nor the }{\b\f210 v}{\f208 e}{\b\f210 lox}{\f208  in P 11: we will see later why these words vary in a different manner.\par }{\b\f210 4. Plaustr}{\f208 u}{\b\f210 mque}{\f208  = }{\b\f210 et pl}{\f208 au}{\b\f210 strum}{\f208 : note the displacement of the accent.\par }{\b\f210 5. Antec}{\f208 e}{\b\f210 do}{\f208 , }{\b\f210 antec}{\f208 e}{\b\f210 dor}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 I pass}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 I am passed}{\f208 ; similarly }{\b\f210 c}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 lloco}{\f208 , }{\b\f210 c}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 llocor}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 I place}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 I am placed}{\f208 .\par }{\b\f210 Active}{\f208  verbs in }{\b\f210 o}{\f208 , }{\b\f210 as}{\f208  (or }{\b\f210 is}{\f208 ), etc., become }{\b\f210 passive}{\f208  (it is the subject which undergoes the action) when their ending is transformed into }{\b\f210 or}{\f208 , }{\b\f210 ris}{\f208 , }{\b\f210 tur}{\f208 , etc. This transformation is very practical. Unfortunately, for some time you will have some difficulties with the spoilsports such as }{\b\f210 l}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 quor}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 I speak}{\f208 ; }{\b\f210 progr}{\f208 e}{\b\f210 dior}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 I advance}{\f208 ; which have exactly the form of the passive verbs, but an active sense (generally intransitive). Be assured that all of this will be clarified before long.\par \par EXERCITATIO\par \par 1.\emdash  Where is the horse?\par 2.\emdash  The horse is in the cart.\par 3.\emdash  Where are the four horses going?\par 4.\emdash  They are going to the theatre.\par 5.\emdash  The quick chariot passes the slow cart.\par 6.\emdash  What do you seek? I seek a fast chariot.\par 7.\emdash  I want to go to Lyon.\par 8.\emdash  [My] wife does not put her suitcases in the car.\par }{\b\f210 \par }{\i\f211 In the exercises you will meet more and more new forms and new words. Do not try to analyze them, and content yourself with getting used to them, helping yourself with the translation.}{\par }}